  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 348 PM CDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nDominant upper ridge remains firmly in place over the western\ntwo thirds of the CONUS This has been the feature holding high\nheat impacting our region for the past several weeks. This is\ndriving today's heat impacts.\n\nAdditionally, increasing overall moisture within the ridge \nahead of a shortwave trough and surface front in western ND is \nbringing chance of widely scattered showers and storms into\nportions of northeast ND and near the international border this\nevening into early Thursday morning.\n\nTomorrow, the upper ridge's crest weakens as one or more \nshortwave troughs will move out of California into the Northern\nPlains and Upper Midwest. This along with a cold front will move\nthrough our area west to east Thursday, supplying medium to high\nchances for showers and storms. Some of these storms may be\nstrong to severe, bringing isolated instances of small hail and\nstrong wind gusts as well as heavy rainfall.\n\nFriday into the weekend will be relatively milder compared to\nrecent stretch of hot conditions, with temperatures closer to\naverage behind Thursday's cold front as well as relatively lower\ndew points.\n\nThis changes come Sunday and Monday, however, as a seasonally\nstrong upper shortwave trough moves across the southern tier of\nCanada. The strong forcing and kinematics ensemble guidance is\npresenting lends confidence toward a return of higher moisture\nand instability into the region. This may set the stage for our\nnext round of strong to severe storms, with some AI/ML guidance\nalready hinting at this potential. At this time, there is too\nmuch uncertainty to comment on further details.\n\n...Heat impacts this afternoon...\n\nExpanded the Heat Advisory for this afternoon across all of \neastern ND as well as northwest and west-central MN. This is due\nto widespread heat indices into the upper 90s and low 100s \narea-wide. This is a result of higher than previously forecast \ndew points currently in place. Additionally, wet bulb globe \ntemperatures into the \"Extreme\" (i.e. Level 5 out of 5) category\nare widespread across the region this afternoon, owing not only\nto high temperatures and humidity, but also mostly sunny skies \nand light winds.\n\nThis should be the last day for potential heat impacts as a cold\nfront moves through tomorrow, in addition to heaping cloud\ncover.\n\n...Strong to severe storms Thursday...\n\nAs the cold front sags into eastern ND and the northern Red\nRiver Valley Thursday morning, thunderstorms are expected to\ndevelop as early as mid morning. Additional thunderstorms will\npercolate and merge with each other given increasing forcing for\nascent as well as overall weak flow within the cloud baring\nlayer - the exception to weak flow is in the upper portions of\nthe cloud layer which may help vent storms and provide some \nhail generation, although not anticipating very large hail with\nthis activity (generally not anticipating hail greater than 1\ninch).\n\nWhile overall flow is weak, high lower level moisture and high\ntemperatures along with cooling temperatures aloft are\ncontributing to DCAPE values exceeding 1000 J/kg. This will\ndrive potential for strong gusts to 60 mph, and would be more\nlikely to occur should outflow from cluster/s of storms\nconsolidate.\n\nConvective rates of at least 1 inch per hour will bring the\npotential for heavy rain leading to flash flooding, particularly\nover more vulnerable landscapes like urban areas and poor\ndrainage areas. This is driven by relatively slow storm \nmotions of thunderstorms amid rich moisture and high instability,\nand potential for training storms. Relatively higher chance for\nthis to occur resides in the southern Red River Valley into\nwest-central MN. With ongoing very dry antecedent conditions,\nrural landscapes will likely not be as vulnerable to flooding\ncompared to urban landscapes.",
